patch(b+'sea-api-design.md',[
('Keyword-only arguments also let you **reorder or add** options later without breaking callers.', 'Keyword-only arguments allow reordering without changing ordinary keyword calls. Adding optional arguments can preserve existing behavior; required additions, subclasses, wrappers and introspection need compatibility checks.'),
('a common incident cause.', 'so callers should choose explicit connect/read timeouts and any required overall deadline.'),
('lets callers catch everything from your library in one clause, without catching unrelated bugs.', 'lets callers catch the expected library errors placed under that hierarchy. Programmer errors and unrelated exceptions may still propagate.'),
('(retry a timeout, show a message for a decline).', '(apply a safe idempotent retry policy for an uncertain timeout, show an appropriate message for a decline).'),
('Wrap it: `raise GatewayTimeout() from exc`.', 'Translate it into a semantically appropriate library error and preserve chaining. Do not label every OperationalError a timeout; failures such as unavailable storage or invalid connection settings differ.'),
('So much code came to rely on it that Python 3.7 made it a language guarantee.', 'Python 3.7 subsequently made insertion order a language guarantee; this fact alone does not establish a single historical cause for the decision.'),
('so nobody can depend on it.', 'to discourage reliance on one stable order; this does not logically prevent callers from depending on observed behavior.'),
('That\'s why so many popular libraries stay at 0.x for years; it lets them break things without a major bump, at the cost of giving users no compatibility signal.', 'SemVer itself does not promise public-API stability during 0.x. Projects can document stricter compatibility policies; no particular maintainer motive or prevalence is inferred here.'),
('    if "secs" in kw:\n        warnings.warn(', '    if "secs" in kw:\n        if timeout is not None:\n            raise TypeError("conflicting times")\n        warnings.warn('),
('        timeout = kw.pop("secs")\n    ...', '        timeout = kw.pop("secs")\n    if kw:\n        raise TypeError("unknown option")\n    return timeout  # boundary demo, no I/O'),
('How long to wait? CPython\'s own policy, PEP 387, requires a warning to appear in at least two minor releases before removal and prefers about five years.', 'How long to wait? CPython\'s PEP 387 normally requires a deprecation period of at least two years across minor releases and prefers five years, with specified exceptions and additional replacement-support guidance.'),
('Ship a `py.typed` marker file (PEP 561) so type checkers use your annotations.', 'For a distributed package containing inline types, ship a py.typed marker as described by PEP 561. Stub-only packages and local source checking follow different discovery rules.')], 'Qualify compatibility/typing claims, classify errors accurately and reject ambiguous deprecated-argument input.',src)
def api(q):
 q[2]['workedExample']=q[2]['workedExample'].replace('you can add or reorder keyword options later without breaking anyone.', 'reordering keyword parameters preserves ordinary calls; adding required options or changing behavior can still break clients.')
 q[3]['workedExample']=q[3]['workedExample'].replace('you can reword messages freely.', 'callers have a stable alternative to parsing messages, though existing text-dependent clients still need migration.')
 q[4]['options'][0]['explanation']='Exposing driver-specific errors couples callers to that driver. Translate expected storage failures into your public hierarchy, with distinctions that accurately describe the cause and retryability.'
 q[4]['workedExample']+='\nOperationalError covers multiple failures: this simplified wrapper must not imply that every such error is transient or safe to retry.'
 q[8]['prompt']+=' Assume parameter names exposed through introspection are not part of the documented public contract.'
 q[8]['options'][3]['explanation']='Introspection changes. Under the stated contract ordinary calls remain compatible; a library that explicitly promises signature metadata would need to consider that promise too.'
 q[9]['options'][2]['explanation']='Keyword-only options improve call clarity. Optional additions can preserve behavior, but no initial default or signature style guarantees that future changes will be compatible.'
 q[9]['options'][3]['explanation']='A stable error hierarchy can decouple callers from transport types, provided the replacement preserves promised error semantics and behavior.'
 q[10]['options'][2]['text']='Allowed by SemVer\'s initial-development rule, unless the project promises stricter compatibility; users should lock or constrain versions and inspect changes.'
 q[10]['workedExample']=q[10]['workedExample'].replace('pin to `~=0.27.0`-style ranges (or lock exact versions)', 'use an exact lock for artifact selection; ~=0.27.0 permits later 0.27 patch releases and is a range rather than an exact pin')
questions(b+'sea-api-design.questions.json',api,'Distinguish API call compatibility from introspection guarantees and ranges from exact pins.',src)
